--TEST--
`Core\Reflect\MethodInfo::parameters` answers one `Core\Reflect\ParameterInfo` per declared parameter, in the order the declaration writes them — `rule:core-classes/reflect`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Reflect\MethodInfo::parameters
// covers: Core\Reflect\ParameterInfo::name
// `rule:core-classes/reflect`: a parameter's name is part of the program's
// shape, so it is introspection and always available — a `private` method
// publishes its parameter names exactly as a `public` one does, and neither
// answer reaches a value.
class Mailer {
    public function constructor(private string $from) {}

    public function send(string $to, string $subject, int $retries): string {
        return $to;
    }

    private function trace(string $note): string {
        return $note;
    }
}

var $described = Core\Reflect::forObject(new Mailer("clerk@example.test"));

// Name order for the methods, declaration order within each one. The promoted
// constructor parameter is a parameter like any other and answers under the
// name its property carries.
foreach ($described->methods() as Core\Reflect\MethodInfo $method) {
    echo $method->name(), ":";
    foreach ($method->parameters() as Core\Reflect\ParameterInfo $parameter) {
        echo " ", $parameter->name();
    }
    echo "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
constructor: from
send: to subject retries
trace: note
